snif NEWS -- history of user-visible changes

Version 1.12
------------

* snifd: a connector whose TLS handshake with the relay fails, or that
  has no usable certificate when it connects, reconnects after 15 s.
  Until now it closed the relay connection for good and kept running
  without one until restarted, so one refused or interrupted handshake,
  a relay restart among them, could leave a device unreachable. A
  connector the relay disconnects without answering now waits before
  reconnecting, 15 s and then twice as long each time up to 5 minutes,
  instead of reconnecting at once in a loop; one the relay had answered
  on reconnects at once, as before. snifd/test/reconnect.sh covers both.

* ca-proxy: retention. snif-arc removes old CA proxy state, for each
  period set in snif.conf; unset, it keeps everything, as before.
  SNIF_ARC_DEVICE_DAYS removes an approved device that long after its
  newest certificate expired; SNIF_ARC_UNAPPROVED_DAYS a name never
  approved, that long after it was allocated; SNIF_ARC_OLD_DAYS archived
  certificates other than each name's newest. SNIF_ARC_AUTH_DAYS clears
  the account an approval names, keeping the approval, and drops old
  approval-counter entries, so the relay keeps who approved a device only
  for that long and never needs to hear of an account's deletion. Each run
  reports its counts in one line. ca-proxy/test/arc.t covers it.

* ca-proxy: snif-syncd's reconciliation leaves alone a difference in which
  every copy is older than SNIF_CLUSTER_RECONCILE_DAYS (default 7, 0 for
  none), and counts it as old= in its reply. Such a difference is what
  snif-arc leaves between two nodes' runs, and repairing it brought back a
  certificate archived, or a record removed, on the node that ran first.
  Writes still travel through the queue; a node down for longer than the
  horizon catches up with snif-syncd seed. test/syncd.t covers it, with
  snif-arc and reconciliation end to end.

Version 1.11
------------

* ca-proxy: snif-arc runs from a systemd timer, snif-arc.timer, daily at
  04:17 as snif; a run missed while the host was down runs at the next
  boot. The install puts it in with the other units and, like them, does
  not enable it: enable it on every relay node (systemctl enable --now
  snif-arc.timer). The cron entry is no longer installed, so a host
  needs no cron daemon: make install-cron puts /etc/cron.d/snif-arc in,
  where absent, for a host without systemd. On a host without a cron
  daemon the entry ran nothing, and certificates were served past 75
  days instead of renewed. An entry an older install put in /etc/cron.d
  is kept and the install reports it: keep the timer or the entry, not
  both.

* ca-proxy: snif-syncd can take a judge, SNIF_CLUSTER_JUDGE=
  <zone>@<server>[#<port>], so that a relay cluster keeps enrolling
  devices while one node is down or cut off. A CSR then needs a majority
  of the nodes and the judge together, two of three for two nodes;
  without the setting it needs every listed peer, as before. The judge
  is a DNS zone on one nameserver, written with TSIG (the key in
  etc/<zone>.tsig; Net::DNS needed): its vote is a TXT record for the
  name, added only where none exists, so of two nodes that cannot reach
  each other exactly one gets a name. It is asked only while a peer does
  not answer. A CSR accepted without a peer is queued for it and
  delivered when the peer is back; until then that node's snifdog
  reports the name. The zone name may start with an underscore.
  doc/cluster.md 5.1 has the rules for the zone: on one server only,
  never copied to a server that can be promoted, never reset.

* snifdog: make install-cron puts the cron entry in /etc/cron.d/snifdog,
  where absent, to run snifdog from cron instead of the snifdog@ units.

Version 1.10
------------

* ca-proxy: snifd runs as its own user, snifd, in group snif only. It
  reads snif.conf and owns its pipes, and can write nothing else; it ran
  as snif, which owns the CA proxy's keys and tree. Group snif is now
  read-only: /etc/snif and etc/ (2750, was 2770). Writing the tree and
  log/ takes the new group snif-rw, and snifd's pipes, which are relay
  control, the new group snif-ctl, which the PHP's user is not in. The
  install makes snifd, snif-rw and snif-ctl, copies a new .tsig as 0600
  snif (it was 0640 root, read through group snif), and reports a key in
  etc/ whose owner or mode is not as ca-proxy/README, "Users and groups",
  says. Run as root, mkdirs now also sets the owner, group and mode of
  what exists, and the install runs it after its chown -R, which had
  taken snifd's pipes from snifd.

* ca-proxy: the PHP endpoints get a user of their own, snif-www (group
  snif, in snif-rw), made by the install, with an example PHP-FPM pool in
  etc/php-fpm/snif.conf. Run them in it rather than as the web server's
  user, which the README had added to snif, so that no other PHP on the
  host can write the tree. On every run the install puts snif, snifd,
  snif-apnsd, snif-www and an existing snif-peer in their groups, and
  reports any other member of snif-rw or snif-ctl.

  Upgrading a running relay, one node at a time: install; make the etc/
  keys 0600 snif, apns.key 0600 snif-apnsd and turnstile.secret 0640
  group snif-rw; then at once restart snif-relay, snif-certd, snif-syncd,
  the snif-tunnel@ units and snif-apnsd where they run, and reload
  PHP-FPM, since a process takes a new group only when it starts. Until
  then the PHP and the peers' sessions cannot write, snifd cannot reopen
  an input pipe, nor snif-apnsd its pipe. A PHP that runs as the web
  server's user needs moving to snif-www, or that user in snif and
  snif-rw.

* ca-proxy: snif-apnsd runs as its own user, snif-apnsd, in its own group
  and snif. It reads snifd's pushes, which carry what connectors send,
  and held every CA proxy key as snif. Now it reads only its pipe
  (snifd-out-apnsd.ctl, group snif-apnsd) and the APNs key, and writes
  only apns/ and each sync-queue/<peer>/apns/, which mkdirs makes for it.
  It logs to its log file where it can write it, else to stderr, which
  is the journal as snif-apnsd; and it logs a wake token it cannot queue
  for a peer, which it dropped silently. Without snif-rw it does not wake
  snif-syncd, which sends the token at its next queue scan, within a
  minute.

* snifdog: runs as its own user, snifdog, and owns its state, which no
  group can write any more (pins/ and the state directory were 2775
  group snif). On a relay host it is in group snif, for snif.conf, its
  configurations and etc/*.domains; a standalone watcher gets its own
  group. An install over an older one gives the state to snifdog.

* snifdog: the install configures and runs nothing. A first install put
  an example /etc/snif/snifdog.conf, which watches snif.xyz, and
  /etc/cron.d/snifdog, which ran at once; both now go to
  /usr/share/snifdog/ as examples (snifdog.conf.example,
  cron.d-snifdog.example). Write one configuration per base in
  /etc/snif/snifdog.d/, then enable snifdog@<base>, or copy the cron
  entry. Files an older install put in /etc are kept: remove the cron
  entry if you use the units, or change its user to snifdog.

* ca-proxy: on a node of a relay cluster, the init origin answered 503
  for a name that cannot be a hostname, such as the paths scanners try
  (/.env, /admin/.env): it asked snif-syncd, which refuses such a path,
  and took the refusal for peers it could not ask. snif-auth.php now
  checks the name first, as snif-cert.php does, and answers 404, as a
  single relay always has; URL text that is not a name, a decoded
  newline included, no longer reaches snif-syncd.

Version 1.09
------------

* relay: snifd crashed at start when an output FIFO (-p) had no reader:
  a cluster node's tunnel FIFOs until its tunnels start, which their
  units order after the relay, or snifd-out-apnsd.ctl on a relay that
  runs no snif-apnsd. Such a FIFO is now retried from the start, and
  keeps what is pushed to it, up to its buffer, until a reader opens it,
  as it already did after a reader went away. Present since the first
  release; a heap that happened to hold zeros hid it.

* relay: a connector's SNIF MSG for its own name also reaches the
  relay's other control connections for that name, never the sender.
  It went only to the output FIFOs, so other connectors with the name
  got it only through a peer node of a cluster. The relay accepts more
  than one connection for a name, which can be the same device back
  after a network problem, and such connectors learn of each other
  through MSG. Connectors need no change: libsnif passes the line to
  the app's callback.

* ca-proxy: snif-certd issues an init origin's certificate for its own
  name ("SNIF CERT <origin>", from csr/<origin>.csr), always from Let's
  Encrypt, by DNS-01 with the base domain's .tsig, else HTTP-01, never
  from a local CA. It asked for *.<origin>, which does not cover the
  origin. In a relay cluster the origin's certificate goes through the
  certificate lease and the sync like any other.

* ca-proxy: snif-syncd listed nothing in a class directory that is a
  symlink, such as crt -> public_html/snif-cert in a tree from the first
  release, so reconciliation and seeding skipped its certificates. It
  now follows the link.

* ca-proxy: snif-apnsd takes the iOS app's bundle ID from
  SNIF_APNS_BUNDLE, and the development environment from
  SNIF_APNS_DEV=1, in snif.conf; the script held sample values that
  snif.conf could not change. Without SNIF_APNS_BUNDLE it stores and
  syncs wake tokens and sends no pushes, so set it before this release
  replaces a snif-apnsd that sends.

* ca-proxy: in a relay cluster, the queue directories the PHP made for a
  peer (sync-queue/<peer>/cfg, auth, ...) were writable by the web
  server's user alone (umask 0022), so snif-syncd, another user, could
  not take entries out of them: names allocated and approvals given on
  one node never reached the others, and a connector polling another
  node for its certificate got 401 for good. The PHP now makes them
  group-writable. On a node that already has them, make the ones the web
  server's user owns group-writable (chmod 2775).

* ca-proxy: a certificate request, or an approval, on a CA proxy where no
  snif-certd was reading var/snif-certd.ctl waited in the PHP's open of
  the pipe until the web server killed it, holding a server process
  each; a connector polling for its certificate kept adding more. The
  pipe is now opened non-blocking: the certificate request gets 502 at
  once, and the approval its 202.

Version 1.08
------------

* libsnif: snif_cert_reset() freed the root store libsnif had made but
  left .rootstore pointing at it. snif_cert_init_ex() on the same struct
  then kept the freed store, and so did snifd's own control connection
  restart, which resets the struct and uses it again; the next
  verification used freed memory. Reset now clears .rootstore, and a new
  store is made from the default paths at init or on the next use. A
  root store the caller set is left as set.

* libsnif: a certificate chain loaded from .certfile was used without
  being verified. It is now verified as a downloaded one is: against
  .rootstore, the private key and the CN; its expiry is still judged by
  snif_cert_ctx(), which renews ahead of it. A chain that fails is
  refused (SNIF_CE_CERT), and a new certificate is downloaded within
  SNIF_CERT_RETRY seconds; its CN still names the host to renew.
  A snifd connector whose certificate comes from a root outside the
  system's default paths needs that root in SSL_CERT_FILE or
  SSL_CERT_DIR, as renewals already did. No interface change; the
  libtool version is 0:2:0.

* install: the ca-proxy install stopped on a fresh host at the chown of
  /var/log/snif, which nothing had made, before the code, the runtime
  directories, the pipes and the units. It now completes, and runs mkdirs,
  which makes crt/ and each cluster peer's pipes and queue as well.

* install: installing the CA proxy again replaced etc/snif.xyz.tsig and
  etc/snif.snif.xyz.domains with the placeholders. Example files in etc/
  are now copied only where absent; snif.conf already was. snifdog's
  example configuration and cron.d entry go in only on a first install,
  so a configuration moved into snifdog.d, or a cron entry removed for the
  watch units, is not brought back.

* install: snif-relay and snif-tunnel moved to ca-proxy/bin/, beside
  their units, and are installed by the ca-proxy install only. Both run
  against the CA proxy tree's pipes. The top-level `make install` is
  libsnif, snifd and snif-conn with its unit; it installed snif-relay and
  snif-tunnel on connector hosts too, and the ca-proxy install installed
  snif-relay a second time. A relay host runs `make install`, then the
  ca-proxy install, as before.

* install: the snif user is a system user with no login and home
  /var/lib/snif, made by any of the three installs, run as root, when it
  does not exist. An existing snif user is left as it is.

* install: every hook honours DESTDIR. Only a root install without
  DESTDIR makes the user, chowns to it, puts units in the system and
  reloads systemd, so an unprivileged install, such as Homebrew's, works
  as before. Code directories of the CA proxy
  that the tree links elsewhere are left alone. ca-proxy/mkdirs leaves
  what exists, takes the base dir as an argument, and run as root gives
  what it makes to snif.

* ca-proxy: snif-certd, snif-apnsd and snif-arc read only /etc/snif.conf,
  while the install and snif-relay use /etc/snif/snif.conf, so on a host
  with only the latter they ran with no CA proxy tree. They
  now read /etc/snif/snif.conf, else /etc/snif.conf, as snif-syncd does.
  snif-arc takes SNIF_CA_PROXY from the environment like the daemons,
  and refuses to run without a tree instead of making /arc.

* ca-proxy: the install adds /etc/cron.d/snif-arc, daily, where absent.
  snif-arc's archiving is what lets a certificate renew, and nothing
  scheduled it. It also adds /etc/logrotate.d/snif for log_snif-certd,
  log_snif-apnsd and log_snif-syncd, which grew without limit; not for
  log/<domain>.log, whose modification time is snif-certd's retry
  throttle.

* units: all wait for network-online.target; the relay, snif-certd,
  snif-apnsd and the connector restart on failure; the relay-side daemons
  run with NoNewPrivileges.

Version 1.07
------------

* libsnif: the control-message builders (snif_conn_start, _forward,
  _reject, _abuse, _msg) compared strlen() with an int length minus a
  margin, so a buffer smaller than the margin passed the check and
  sprintf() wrote past it. They now return 0 for such a buffer.
  snif_conn_abuse() also reserves room for an 11-character score
  (-2147483648). curl_easy_setopt() gets a long for CURLOPT_VERBOSE,
  CURLOPT_UPLOAD and CURLOPT_INFILESIZE. snifd always passed large enough
  buffers, so it was not affected. No interface change; the libtool
  version is 0:1:0.

* relay: a CONNECT read from an input FIFO for a device that has no
  control connection here is held instead of dropped, and sent when the
  device connects, after this relay's own waiting clients. A held CONNECT
  is dropped on the client's CLEAR or CLOSE, or at the client timeout; at
  most SNIF_HOST_MAXHELD (4096) are held, oldest dropped first. This lets
  a client on one node of a relay cluster reach a device that connects to
  another node later, for example after an APNs wake.

* snifd: outgoing connections time out after 5 s per address
  (SNIF_SOCK_CONNTMOUT) instead of the kernel's SYN timeout, which
  stalled the whole process for about two minutes on an address that
  drops packets. This covers the connector's control and service
  connections and the relay static map.

* snifd: a -DSNIF_DEBUG build compiles again (stdio.h was missing).

* ca-proxy: a base domain can issue from a local CA (etc/<domain>.pub-ca
  and etc/<domain>.priv-ca) instead of Let's Encrypt, for closed systems
  whose connectors and relay trust a private root. snif-certd signs the
  CSR with the openssl CLI; certificates are wildcards for the device
  zone, valid 90 days, with .pub-ca as the chain. Crypt::LE,
  Crypt::OpenSSL::RSA and Net::DNS are loaded only when needed.
  snif-arc keeps such a domain's published root bundle,
  crt/<domain>.crt, instead of archiving it after 75 days.

* ca-proxy: DNS-01 in snif-certd adds its challenge values instead of
  replacing the record set, so a name and its wildcard, or two attempts
  at once, no longer remove each other's. It follows the alias of
  _acme-challenge.<host>, so several base domains can share one pool
  name, sends each update to every nameserver of the zone with a 5 s
  timeout, waits until every server that answered serves the values, and
  removes exactly its own values afterwards, retrying a failed removal
  for up to 7 days (var/snif-certd.pending).

* relay cluster (doc/cluster.md): several relay nodes can serve one set
  of names. snif-syncd keeps the CA proxy state (allocations, CSRs,
  approvals, certificates, wake tokens, counters) in step across the
  nodes over ssh; bin/snif-tunnel and snif-tunnel@.service carry each
  node's pushes to its peers. The CA proxy, snif-certd and snif-apnsd
  work through it when etc/cluster.node names the node; without it a
  relay behaves as before. See SNIF_CLUSTER_* in etc/snif.conf.

* snif-apnsd: a wake token is replaced by rename, so a reader never sees
  a partial one; its last-wake time is kept.

* snifdog: one configuration per base domain. The cron entry and
  snifdog@.service run /etc/snif/snifdog.conf and every
  /etc/snif/snifdog.d/*.conf; each base gets its own state directory,
  which snifdog refuses to share, and a lock per pass.

* ca-proxy: the VES approval limit is a leaky bucket instead of 11
  approvals per account for life. Each approval adds one and one leaks
  out per refill time; etc/<email>.ves-limit, else etc/ves-limit, sets
  the limit and the refill time (default "10 1d").

Version 1.06
------------

* PROXY protocol v1: a `+` prefix on the destination port of a connector
  port mapping (SNIF_CONN_PORTS, combines with `^`, e.g. `443:+8443` or
  `8080:^+80`) or of a relay static SNI map entry (SNIF_RELAY_MAP /
  `-m ...:+dst_port`) prepends a HAProxy PROXY protocol v1 line
  (`PROXY TCP4|TCP6 src dst sport dport`) to the forwarded stream, so the
  destination service learns the original client address instead of
  seeing every connection as coming from localhost. The destination
  listener must be configured to expect the PROXY header (nginx
  `proxy_protocol`, Apache mod_remoteip, Postfix, Dovecot, HAProxy, ...).
  On the connector the client address is as reported by the relay; on a
  relay map both addresses are taken first-hand from the client socket.

Version 1.05
------------

* relay: static SNI map. `snifd -m sni_host:fwd_port:dst_host:dst_port`
  (repeatable) forwards the still-encrypted TLS stream for a mapped SNI
  hostname arriving on the mapped relay port directly to a fixed
  destination, bypassing connector dispatch; the destination terminates
  TLS with its own certificate. Set SNIF_RELAY_MAP in etc/snif.conf to
  have bin/snif-relay pass the mappings to snifd. A mapped hostname
  arriving on a non-mapped relay port is dropped immediately instead of
  being held.

* relay: the LISTEN wildcard hostname match is rewritten as an anchored
  suffix comparison. The previous length/prefix-based check could accept
  non-matching hostnames and crashed on hostnames containing no dot;
  hostnames with an empty left label (".example.com") are now rejected.

* build: bin/snif-relay is now installed by `make install`.

Version 1.04
------------

* build: fix `make dist` so the release tarball is complete. bin/snif-conn
  (declared bin_SCRIPTS, which automake treats as generated) and the
  etc/snif.conf + etc/systemd/snif-conn.service files consumed by
  install-data-hook were missing from the distribution, breaking a build
  from the tarball (they were present in a git checkout). No functional
  change to the connector or relay.

Version 1.03
------------

* snifdog: new CA Transparency watchdog (M1), a standalone sub-package that
  monitors public CT logs for certificates issued under a SNIF namespace and
  validates them against name-bound CSRs, backstopping the relay-cannot-
  impersonate guarantee. Ships its own configure.ac; see snifdog/README.
  Includes a first-run crt.sh history backfill with incremental checkpointing
  and transient-failure backoff.

* docker: official SNIF Connector container image (Dockerfile + docker/); the
  image is published to GHCR automatically on each version tag.

* cert: the connector key algorithm is now configurable, and the CA proxy may
  steer the choice via an X-SNIF-KeyAlgo response header.

* cert: CA proxy issuance errors are delivered to the connector instead of
  hanging on an endless 503.

* ca-proxy: the Turnstile sitekey is server-pinned; added a mkdirs bootstrap
  script.

Version 1.02
------------

* Live in-browser demo (wasm connector + CA-proxy captcha issuance path),
  comparison table.

Version 1.01
------------

* Relicensed to Apache-2.0; added CI, a security policy, and community docs.
